  "text": "Questions seven and eight. Data drift versus concept drift versus performance degradation? And how do you detect and respond to concept drift? Data drift is the inputs shifting: a new kind of customer, a sensor's new units. Concept drift is the link from input to label shifting: fraud tactics evolve, so the same features mean something new. Degradation is the drop in quality either can cause. Simulate a model trained on two features. Next quarter, the first one shifts. Its drift score, the population stability index or PSI, reads point nine one: a loud alarm. Yet accuracy rises, from eighty percent to eighty-four. This time, the rule the model learned still held where the inputs moved. Another quarter, and no input moves at all. Every PSI, on both features and on the predictions, stays under point oh two. But the relationship changed, and accuracy falls to sixty-one. The inputs rang for the harmless shift and stayed silent for the harmful one. Confirming concept drift takes labels: as they arrive, track accuracy and calibration over time, against stable cohorts where you can. Then respond in proportion: an alert, a retrain on recent data, or new features if the change is structural.",
